diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage of web2.local or web3.local from the reported client. From host_1_1_1_1, both name resolution and HTTP reachability are healthy: getent resolves web2.local → 10.200.0.5 and web3.local → 10.200.0.6 via DNS server 10.200.0.2, and curl to http://web2.local and http://web3.local returns HTTP 200 with sub‑6 ms total time. ICMP from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.5/10.200.0.6 shows 0% loss and low RTT. On the servers, TCP/80 is listening (netstat on web_server_2 and web_server_3 shows 0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN). The DNS server is up and serving on 10.200.0.2:53 (netstat shows named/bind ports). This indicates the server access switch path and core routing are functioning.

Mechanism: If there had been a switch or uplink fault shared by both servers, we would see loss or failures to the IPs, but we observe clean connectivity and valid HTTP responses end-to-end. DNS also resolves correctly.

Localization: No failures found across host_1_1_1_1, DNS (10.200.0.2), web_server_2 (10.200.0.5), or web_server_3 (10.200.0.6), including the shared switch_server_access segment.

Root cause: Not currently reproducible; services and network are operational. This may have been a transient client-side or DNS caching issue that has since cleared.

Corrective fix: No change required now. If it recurs, capture “dig web2.local web3.local”, “curl -v”, and timestamps; also check for client browser/cache issues and switch_server_access port errors. Monitoring recommended. 
